{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Boston, Concord and Montreal Railroad (BC&M) was the railroad that opened New Hampshire's Lakes Region and White Mountains to tourism and industry -- the biggest single gap in this map's New Hampshire coverage before now. Chartered 1844, its main line ran Concord (connecting there with the nh_concord_northern_railroads.geojson corridor to Nashua/Lowell/Manchester) north through Tilton, Laconia, Lakeport, Meredith, and Plymouth to Warren and Woodsville/Wells River (opened in stages 1848-1853), where it met railroads continuing toward Vermont and Montreal. Three branches are also included: the White Mountains Railroad (Woodsville-Littleton-Groveton), the Pemigewasset Valley Railroad (Plymouth-North Woodstock-Lincoln, serving Franconia Notch), and the Whitefield and Jefferson Railroad (Whitefield-Jefferson-Berlin). The BC&M merged with the Concord Railroad in 1889, was leased to the Boston and Maine in 1895, and formally merged into the B&M in 1919; passenger service across this whole network had ended by the mid-1960s, matching the broader B&M passenger contraction described in nh_concord_northern_railroads.geojson. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap (active rail combined with abandoned/disused railway tagging, since almost the entire network has been out of scheduled passenger service for 60+ years); every hop resolved to a connected, plausible real track path with no straight-line fallbacks needed anywhere on any of the four lines.", "sources": ["https://archive.org/stream/bostonconcordmon00casw/bostonconcordmon00casw_djvu.txt (Boston, Concord & Montreal: story of the building and early days of this road -- primary-source station-by-station opening dates and mileage for the main line and all three branches)", "https://www.laconiadailysun.com/community/lake_style/celebrate-laconia-the-boston-concord-montreal-railroad-came-to-town-bringing-prosperity-with-it/article_5ca333ee-012d-507e-af71-8c4cf3dd64a0.html (Laconia route confirmation)", "https://www.nashuacitystation.org/history/the-concord-and-montreal-railroad/ (1889 merger with the Concord Railroad forming the Concord and Montreal Railroad; 1895 Boston and Maine lease; 1919 formal B&M merger)"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API, overpass.openstreetmap.fr mirror): railway=rail (active) combined with railway=abandoned/disused tagging across the whole Concord-Woodsville-Groveton-Lincoln-Berlin area; routed via Dijkstra shortest path through a graph of real track ways between each station's geocoded (Nominatim/OSM) coordinates. All hops on all four lines resolved to connected, plausible paths (distance ratios within ~1.3x of straight-line) with no implausible-detour fallbacks or disconnections."], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; the precise year the Concord and Northern Railroads (see nh_concord_northern_railroads.geojson) and this network's various components were formally absorbed into Boston and Maine ownership vs. just leased/controlled; whether passenger service on the three branches ended at the same time as the main line.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Boston, Concord and Montreal Railroad", "line": "Boston, Concord and Montreal: Concord \u2013 Woodsville", "id": "bcm-concord-woodsville", "color": "#2a9d8f", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated: mid-1800s pioneer-era steam rail through the Lakes Region; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1848, "end": 1853, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Chartered 1844, construction began at Concord in 1846. Opened in stages: Concord-Sanbornton Bridge (Tilton), 18 miles, May 10 1848; to Meredith Bridge (Laconia) in August 1848; to Lake Village (Lakeport, where the repair shops were located) in October 1848; to Meredith Village March 19 1849; to Fogg's Hill (New Hampton) July 1849; to Plymouth January 1850 (51 miles); to Warren June 1851; and to its terminus at Wells River/Woodsville May 10 1853 -- 93 miles from Concord. This railroad and its branches opened the Lakes Region and White Mountains to tourism. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://archive.org/stream/bostonconcordmon00casw/bostonconcordmon00casw_djvu.txt", "https://www.laconiadailysun.com/community/lake_style/celebrate-laconia-the-boston-concord-montreal-railroad-came-to-town-bringing-prosperity-with-it/article_5ca333ee-012d-507e-af71-8c4cf3dd64a0.html", "https://www.nashuacitystation.org/history/the-concord-and-montreal-railroad/"]}, {"start": 1853, "end": 1889, "headway_min": 300, "note": "Independent operation era, connecting at Woodsville/Wells River with railroads reaching further into Vermont and toward Montreal. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://archive.org/stream/bostonconcordmon00casw/bostonconcordmon00casw_djvu.txt", "https://www.laconiadailysun.com/community/lake_style/celebrate-laconia-the-boston-concord-montreal-railroad-came